# The office server is Ubuntu; development is Windows. A shell script or systemd
# unit checked out with CRLF fails on Ubuntu with the near-undebuggable
# `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`. These rules make that impossible rather than
# merely unlikely, and tools/check_text_hygiene.py enforces them in CI.
#
# The catch-all comes first and deliberately covers everything, including files
# with no extension and extensions nobody thought to list. An earlier version of
# this file enumerated suffixes and silently missed .gitignore and
# systemd/service.template, both of which git was about to rewrite to CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Listed explicitly as well, so the intent survives an edit to the line above.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bats          text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.service.in    text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
VERSION         text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf

# Release archives are built by GitHub Actions on Linux and are never to be
# rebuilt or re-committed from Windows.
*.tar.gz        binary
*.sha256        text eol=lf
*.template     text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
